From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 00:22:19 +0200
Subject: leds: lgm-sso: take the shift clock rate from the device tree

SSO_CON1.FCDSC divides the block's 25 MHz source down to the rate the
output word is clocked out to the external shift register on: 25, 12.5,
6.25 or 3.125 MHz. How slow a rate a board needs is a property of that
shift register and of the trace to it, so it can only come from the
device tree -- and the driver has the plumbing for it, a table and a
lookup, with nothing wired to the input. sso_probe_gpios() hardcodes
priv->gpio.shift_clk_freq to -1, which no table entry is below, so
sso_register_shift_clk() takes its error path on every board it has ever
run on: it warns "Invalid freq -1" and then programs the field to 0.

Read it from an optional property instead. The name follows the
intel,sso-update-rate-hz already in this binding, and the four rates are
spelled out as an enum because the divider admits no others.

Absent the property the field is still programmed to 0, so no board's
hardware behaviour changes; what stops is the warning, which was never
about the DT and only ever reported the driver's own placeholder. A
value out of range still takes the same path and now says something
true.

The AVM FRITZ!Box 7580, a GRX550 board, is the first user: the vendor
device tree asks for 3.125 MHz on it while every other board of that
family leaves the default standing.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
index a5416d4ba19a..4cf5aa2cb92b 100644
--- a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/leds/leds-lgm.yaml
@@ -53,6 +53,15 @@ properties:
     description:
       Blink frequency for SOUTs in Hz.
 
+  intel,sso-shift-clk-frequency-hz:
+    description:
+      Frequency in Hz of the clock the output word is shifted out on. The
+      controller derives it from a 25 MHz source through a fixed divider of
+      1, 2, 4 or 8, so only those four rates exist. How slow a rate a board
+      needs depends on its shift register and on the trace to it.
+    enum: [3125000, 6250000, 12500000, 25000000]
+    default: 25000000
+
   ssoled:
     type: object
     description:
diff --git a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
index 68e367cb4888..25725a4a330f 100644
--- a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
@@ -801,7 +801,18 @@ static int sso_probe_gpios(struct sso_led_priv *priv)
 		priv->gpio.freq = 0;
 
 	priv->gpio.edge = priv->variant->data_clk_edge;
-	priv->gpio.shift_clk_freq = -1;
+
+	/*
+	 * How fast the output word is clocked out to the external shift
+	 * register. SSO_CON1.FCDSC divides a 25 MHz source by 1, 2, 4 or 8,
+	 * and how slow a rate a board needs is a property of that register and
+	 * of the trace between -- nothing the SoC can be asked about. Absent
+	 * the property, ask for the top of the table, which is the divider
+	 * setting the field has always been left at.
+	 */
+	if (device_property_read_u32(dev, "intel,sso-shift-clk-frequency-hz",
+				     &priv->gpio.shift_clk_freq))
+		priv->gpio.shift_clk_freq = shift_clk_freq_tbl[0];
 
 	ret = sso_gpio_hw_init(priv);
 	if (ret)
